WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.434
This morning Boeing.

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.302
737

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:04.804
max nine jets are all grounded,

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.339
meaning hundreds of flights

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:07.774
forced to be canceled

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.275
as investigators are urgently

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:10.143
trying to figure out

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:13.313
why a big chunk of an Alaska Airlines

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:15.281
plane tore off mid-flight.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:16.383
A huge hole

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:18.418
and a ton of questions left behind.

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:19.786
Some of the new details today

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:21.021
and the month before.

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:22.122
Friday's terrifying.

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:23.556
20 minutes in the air.

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:24.591
A pressurization

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:27.260
warning light had gone off three times.

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:28.995
That led Alaska Airlines

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:30.797
to actually restrict the plane

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:31.531
from flying over

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:32.165
water on long

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:32.632
haul trips

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:33.433
to ensure that

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:35.335
it could return quickly to an airport

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:36.436
if necessary.

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.871
On Friday, passengers

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:40.140
describe a violent explosion

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.741
when the plane's door plug

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:42.976
blew off shortly

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:44.310
after takeoff from Portland, Oregon.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.413
And then headrests were torn off.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:47.280
Extra oxygen.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.382
Oxygen masks were sheared off.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:51.618
Some seats were even twisted

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:53.920
from the force of the depressurization.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.188
One passenger says a child's

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:57.357
shirt was ripped off from it.

00:00:57.357 --> 00:00:59.292
Thankfully, the two seats directly next

00:00:59.292 --> 00:00:59.659
to what

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:00.727
became a massive hole

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:03.463
were not occupied on this plane.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:04.798
Also this morning,

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:05.899
a crucial piece of evidence

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:06.833
has been recovered.

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.368
The refrigerator sized door plugs

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:10.437
found in someone's backyard in Portland,

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:12.005
but another key piece of

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.074
the investigation has been lost.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:15.809
The cockpit voice recording.

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:17.243
The NTSB says it was

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:18.344
completely overwritten

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:20.180
We can

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.381
learn a lot

00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:23.983
from that cockpit voice recorder.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.720
We have urged the FAA to extend

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.721
the cockpit voice

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:30.123
recorder time

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:32.358
from 2 hours to 25 hours

00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:33.960
because we want to hear

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.129
communications, noise

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:37.864
alerts on the flight deck,

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:40.200
which may help us prevent

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.104
future tragedies.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.606
CNN's Mike Valero is in Portland,

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:48.708
CNN aviation correspondent Pete Meehan.

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:50.577
Pete, Manton is in D.C..

00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:52.145
Pete, let's start with you.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:52.812
What's the latest

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:53.847
on the investigation now?

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:54.280
Well, Kate,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.748
major developments here about this

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.318
violent, explosive and terrifying event.

00:01:58.351 --> 00:01:59.586
The NTSB

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.221
has inspected the interior

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:03.123
and the exterior of the plane

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.657
featuring that twisted

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.926
and contorted seat metal from that

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:07.961
400 mile

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.095
per hour rush of air

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:09.629
through that

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:11.131
refrigerator sized opening

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:12.532
in the left side of the fuselage.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:13.433
The big development,

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:14.834
this is the smoking gun here.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.170
The piece that fell off of the plane

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.073
has been found NTSB chair Jennifer Hamidi

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:21.975
rushed back to the podium last night

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:22.442
to announce

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:24.210
that found in the backyard

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.678
of a Portland school teacher.

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:27.013
We only know his first name.

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.181
It is Bob

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:30.683
that part is known as a door plug

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.852
and it fills a spot on the plane

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.187
where an optional door

00:02:34.187 --> 00:02:36.723
could be installed at the Boeing factory.

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.158
Finding this is key

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:39.959
because it will hold some major clues.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.995
NTSB chair Jennifer Hammond just told me.

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:43.830
Was this a mechanical failure?

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.798
Was this a manufacturing defect

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.600
by Boeing or its contractors?

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.968
That is the key

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.604
because this airplane is only months old,

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:52.639
took its first flight.

00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:56.643
October 15th, you flew only 150 flights

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.111
for Alaska. Airlines.

00:02:58.111 --> 00:03:00.380
Investigators also now say

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.814
a pressurization warning

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.316
light came on in the plane

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:05.718
three times before this incident.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.320
Once just the day before it.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.755
And that caused Alaska

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:10.190
to restrict this plane

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.491
from long flights over

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:12.992
water like to Hawaii.

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:14.227
Could have been an early warning.

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:15.895
And it's something that investigators

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:18.097
are probing but are still unclear about.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:21.201
That alert

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:23.303
that illuminated those three times

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:24.103
certainly

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.905
is very disconcerting

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:27.040
to our investigators.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.174
And we want to look at that.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:31.678
But it may have absolutely nothing to do

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:33.780
with what occurred

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.848
in the cabin of the aircraft on that

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.084
during that event.

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.986
What is missing from this investigation?

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:40.787
The audio

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:42.422
from the cockpit voice recorder

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:44.457
that was overwritten automatically.

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.859
How many calls that very disappointing

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:48.695
there's been a push to make recorders

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:50.430
retain information for even longer.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.631
25 hours.

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.132
Still a lot of info out there, though.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.734
During the depressurization,

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.603
the cockpit door blew open

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:58.238
investigators have been able to tell that

00:03:58.238 --> 00:04:00.306
also that the pilots headsets

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:01.574
and checklists

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:02.942
were thrown by the force

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:04.444
of that depressurization.

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:06.779
Alaska Airlines and United Airlines

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:08.281
are the only two U.S.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:09.582
airlines operating these Boeing.

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:11.251
Seven 37, max nine.

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:13.219
They are still grounded this morning,

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:15.221
awaiting new details on inspections

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:16.990
mandated by the FAA.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.424
United Airlines canceled

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:21.928
270 max nine flights over the weekend.

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:23.930
I want to play for you.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:24.931
You talked about what

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.132
we're normally talking about

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:26.633
as the search

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:29.902
for the black box, the cockpit voice

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.005
and data recorders to go through that.

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:33.706
One thing we know here

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.042
is that the cockpit voice recorder

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:38.478
very basically have lost

00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:39.979
all of the usable

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:43.483
material from it because of how it gets

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:45.218
overwritten

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:46.653
every 2 hours for the control room.

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:47.687
This is a second sound byte.

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:50.123
Let me play with the chair of the NTSB

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:51.624
said about this today, Miles.

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:54.360
We're

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:56.362
disappointed that the

00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:58.631
cockpit voice recorder was overwritten.

00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:01.000
We can learn a lot from that

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.236
cockpit voice recorder.

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:06.272
We have urged the FAA to

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:09.108
extend the cockpit voice recorder time

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:11.611
from 2 hours to 25 hours

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:13.179
because we want to hear

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:15.381
communications, noise

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:17.116
alerts on the flight deck,

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:19.419
which may help us prevent

00:05:21.254 --> 00:05:23.056
future tragedies.

00:05:23.056 --> 00:05:25.024
Jennifer Hamidi is not happy

00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:26.192
that this happened,

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:27.660
that this went down this way.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.495
What could be on there

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:32.332
that is that now is not Miles.

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:34.200
Yeah, you've hit a pet

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.402
peeve of mine as well, Kate.

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:39.372
To me, in this day and age,

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:40.673
it's kind of scandalous.

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:41.708
That we only capture

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:44.310
the last 2 hours with digital data

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:45.211
and hard drives.

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:45.545
And we don't

00:05:45.545 --> 00:05:47.814
we don't have looped tapes anymore.

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:51.017
We could capture in excess of 24 hours,

00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:51.951
as is recommended.

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:52.952
And I'm going to take it

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:53.853
one step further

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:57.423
from the good chairperson and suggest

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:58.725
once again

00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:00.193
that we have video cameras

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:01.227
in the cockpits.

00:06:01.227 --> 00:06:03.629
The pilots union hates these ideas.

00:06:03.663 --> 00:06:04.397
They consider it

00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:06.632
invasion of privacy, as they say.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.134
Well, I don't know about you,

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:09.001
but when I'm at work,

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:10.703
I don't consider that a private place.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:12.572
And if what you're doing

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:14.107
can involve consequences

00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:15.875
which lead to the deaths of others,

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.410
I think you might have to give up

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.512
a little bit of your so-called privacy.

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:21.948
It's high time that we improved

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:23.416
the amount of data

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:24.417
we got out of these

00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:27.120
cockpit voice recorders, including video

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.254
and for that matter.

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:29.389
It should be streaming

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:30.857
out of the aircraft in real time.

00:06:30.857 --> 00:06:33.626
But that's you got me on my high horse.

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:35.895
All right.
We certainly need to discuss this more.

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:37.230
I'm joined now by airline analyst

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:37.930
John Strickland.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:38.898
He's also the director

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:40.933
of the Air Transport Consulting firm

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.802
JLL Consulting.

00:06:42.802 --> 00:06:44.370
Thank you so much for coming in

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:45.738
and talking about this today.

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:46.305
Look,

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.241
so many of us fly

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:49.275
the seven 37

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:50.643
have been a problem in the past.

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:51.210
They were grounded

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:54.247
I think in 2019 for a year and a half

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:55.481
after a couple of crashes

00:06:55.481 --> 00:06:57.750
involving the max eight aircraft

00:06:58.017 --> 00:07:00.153
that killed 346 people.

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:02.455
I mean can you give us some sense of

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:03.990
is this a different incident?

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:04.991
Is there something else

00:07:04.991 --> 00:07:06.225
wrong with the seven 37?

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:08.428
Is this just a fluke

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:10.129
or is there a major

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:11.931
problem with these seven 37.

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:14.934
Well yes you're right.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.269
The seven 47

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:17.170
Max family

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.070
and at that time

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:18.838
it was

00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:21.707
the Max Series eight which had the two

00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:23.509
tragic fatal crashes

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:25.411
that resulted in that 18 month grounding.

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:27.780
The nine was not in service

00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:29.982
at that point,

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:32.285
but is another member of the same family.

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:36.222
The incident of the fuselage panel

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:39.759
the blocked off optional emergency door

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:41.461
that detached off the Alaska Airlines

00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:42.195
flight is something

00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:43.396
quite different

00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:46.332
to the cause of those two fatal crashes.

00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:48.301
And the reason for the grounding

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:49.902
that was a piece of software

00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:50.837
used by the pilots

00:07:50.837 --> 00:07:51.571
on the flight deck

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:53.739
to be called the Marcus system.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:58.678
It was something which had caused crashes

00:07:58.678 --> 00:08:02.315
and which had to be completely reworked

00:08:02.315 --> 00:08:03.149
before that aircraft

00:08:03.149 --> 00:08:06.018
was able
to get back to being recertified as a

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:07.620
as a safe aircraft

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.088
for commercial operation.

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:10.857
It also, of course, affected

00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:11.824
Boeing as a company,

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:14.293
which lost an enormous amount of trust

00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:16.496
and confidence during that period.

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:17.730
And they had to work hard

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:18.498
to bring that back

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:19.332
and rehabilitate

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:20.466
the aircraft into service.

00:08:20.466 --> 00:08:22.768
So last week's incident quite different.

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:25.204
We don't know what caused it yet.

00:08:25.204 --> 00:08:26.906
I think as one of your reporters

00:08:26.906 --> 00:08:28.508
commented, that it's important.

00:08:28.508 --> 00:08:30.576
This is also quite a new aircraft,

00:08:30.743 --> 00:08:32.345
only about two months off the

00:08:32.345 --> 00:08:33.479
production line.

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:34.313
We wouldn't expect

00:08:34.313 --> 00:08:35.147
something like this

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:36.148
to happen

00:08:36.148 --> 00:08:38.017
on any well-maintained aircraft,

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:39.252
be it old or young.

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:40.920
But certainly it's particularly

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:41.521
surprising

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:44.323
to get this news of a very new aircraft.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:47.059
I don't think it reflects a wider problem

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:49.929
with the 7372737 itself

00:08:50.296 --> 00:08:53.032
as a type goes back to the 1960s.

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:54.600
It's a tried and tested

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.170
aircraft which has been modified updated

00:08:58.771 --> 00:09:00.239
into far more advanced

00:09:00.239 --> 00:09:01.440
technology versions

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:02.475
such as the max that we're

00:09:02.475 --> 00:09:03.643
flying on today.

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:04.410
It's delivered

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.045
millions of hours of service,

00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:07.079
carried millions of people

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:08.014
around the world.

00:09:08.014 --> 00:09:09.015
But that doesn't detract

00:09:09.015 --> 00:09:09.815
from the seriousness

00:09:09.815 --> 00:09:10.883
of this incident of a need

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:12.151
to get to the bottom of it

00:09:12.151 --> 00:09:13.052
as quickly as possible

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:14.453
and keep that

00:09:14.487 --> 00:09:16.789
absolutely essential focus on safety

00:09:17.356 --> 00:09:18.491
Yeah, I mean, I've certainly

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:19.191
and I know my colleagues

00:09:19.191 --> 00:09:19.759
have certainly flown

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:21.928
a lot of seven 30 sevens in our lifetime

00:09:21.928 --> 00:09:22.962
with all the traveling that we do.

00:09:22.962 --> 00:09:24.163
I do want to ask you about something

00:09:24.163 --> 00:09:25.898
the Seattle Times reported that according

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:27.900
to the Alaskan Airlines,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:28.634
in the days

00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:30.570
before this particular incident,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:33.239
pilots had filed several reports

00:09:33.472 --> 00:09:34.607
of warning lights

00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:35.408
that indicated

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:37.476
some sort of loss of cabin pressure

00:09:37.476 --> 00:09:39.845
and that the plane was restricted

00:09:39.845 --> 00:09:41.948
from long distance flight over

00:09:41.948 --> 00:09:43.382
water as a result.

00:09:43.382 --> 00:09:44.250
Should this plane have just

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:45.251
been grounded, period?

00:09:46.719 --> 00:09:47.119
Well, it's

00:09:47.119 --> 00:09:48.120
difficult to comment

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.122
without knowing more than those facts.

00:09:50.122 --> 00:09:51.524
We have heard that

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:53.593
and we heard from the lead

00:09:53.593 --> 00:09:54.660
there in your report

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.529
of the accident investigation team.

00:09:56.529 --> 00:09:57.597
This is something

00:09:57.597 --> 00:10:00.132
of interest of concern to them.

00:10:00.533 --> 00:10:02.702
There can be many warnings

00:10:02.702 --> 00:10:05.171
generated on flights every day.

00:10:05.204 --> 00:10:06.872
To pilots.

00:10:06.872 --> 00:10:08.841
Largely, these are quite routine

00:10:08.841 --> 00:10:10.176
pilots to manage.

00:10:10.176 --> 00:10:11.611
They are reported

00:10:11.611 --> 00:10:13.079
even directly by the pilots

00:10:13.079 --> 00:10:14.246
or more and more these days,

00:10:14.246 --> 00:10:16.449
automatically from data systems,

00:10:16.449 --> 00:10:19.719
from aircraft flight next to the ground

00:10:19.852 --> 00:10:22.455
engineering maintenance control teams.

00:10:22.922 --> 00:10:24.690
It's difficult to say more than that.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:25.124
As to what

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:25.891
the significance

00:10:25.891 --> 00:10:28.327
of those pressurization warnings were.

00:10:28.794 --> 00:10:30.496
It seems potentially

00:10:30.496 --> 00:10:32.064
more than coincidental,

00:10:32.064 --> 00:10:33.132
but it could have been something

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:34.100
very, very minor.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.434
It could have been something

00:10:35.434 --> 00:10:36.302
completely different.

00:10:36.302 --> 00:10:37.703
Pressurization embraces

00:10:37.703 --> 00:10:40.172
a lot of elements on an aircraft, but

00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:41.507
certainly that needs to be

00:10:42.908 --> 00:10:44.176
checked out, worked out

00:10:44.176 --> 00:10:45.878
as quickly as possible.

00:10:45.878 --> 00:10:46.779
Absolutely.

00:10:46.779 --> 00:10:47.847
There's a lot of people

00:10:47.847 --> 00:10:49.849
fear of flying is a real thing,

00:10:49.849 --> 00:10:51.450
and to see this is terrifying.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:52.385
John Strickland,

00:10:52.385 --> 00:10:53.552
thank you so much for giving us

00:10:53.552 --> 00:10:54.353
this analysis.

00:10:54.353 --> 00:10:55.287
This morning. Appreciate you.

